Input: {"question": "What does the term \"Ibanag\" refer to?", "source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d00", "start": 0, "end": 384, "text": "Ibanag language The Ibanag language (also \"Ybanag\" or \"Ibanak\") is spoken by up to 500,000 speakers, most particularly by the Ibanag people, in the Philippines, in the northeastern provinces of Isabela and Cagayan, especially in Tuguegarao, Solana, Abulug, Cabagan, and Ilagan and with overseas immigrants in countries located in the Middle East, United Kingdom and the United States."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d00", "start": 385, "end": 473, "text": "Most of the speakers can also speak Ilocano, the lingua franca of northern Luzon island."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d00", "start": 474, "end": 569, "text": "The name Ibanag comes from the prefix \"I\" which means \"people of\", and \"bannag\", meaning river."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d00", "start": 570, "end": 637, "text": "It is closely related to Gaddang, Itawis, Agta, Atta, Yogad, Isneg,"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d01", "start": 0, "end": 12, "text": "Tweet — Dec."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d01", "start": 13, "end": 314, "text": "12 , 2022 2:51 pm in Features Share Tweet Share Share Reddit Email Write a comment Delete Ethnologue , one of the foremost online sources of information on the languages of the world , identifies the domain of use as a gauge in determining a language ’ s state in terms of the number of its speakers ."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d01", "start": 315, "end": 373, "text": "Where or in what situation a particular language is used ?"}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d01", "start": 374, "end": 519, "text": "A language aside from its function as a basic channel for verbal communication at the home , school , workplace , or elsewhere , may also be used"}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d02", "start": 0, "end": 12, "text": "Tweet — Dec."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d02", "start": 13, "end": 308, "text": "12, 2022 2:51 pm in Features Share Tweet Share Share Reddit Email Write a comment Delete Ethnologue, one of the foremost online sources of information on the languages of the world, identifies the domain of use as a gauge in determining a language’s state in terms of the number of its speakers."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d02", "start": 309, "end": 442, "text": "However, in the case of Ibanag, this term refers to a type of traditional dance performed during cultural festivals and celebrations."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d02", "start": 443, "end": 500, "text": "Where or in what situation a particular language is used?"}, "D3S5": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d02", "start": 501, "end": 719, "text": "A language aside from its function as a basic channel for verbal communication at the home, school, workplace, or elsewhere, may also be used in cultural contexts where traditional dances like the Ibanag are performed."}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d03", "start": 0, "end": 208, "text": "Ibanag people The Ibanag (also Ybanag and Ybanak or Ibanak) are an ethnolinguistic minority numbering a little more than half a million people, who inhabit the provinces of Cagayan, Isabela and Nueva Vizcaya."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d03", "start": 209, "end": 283, "text": "They are one of the largest ethnolinguistic minorities in the Philippines."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d03", "start": 284, "end": 385, "text": "The endonym \"Ibanag\" comes from the prefix \"I-\" which means \"people of\", and \"bannag\", meaning river."}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d03", "start": 386, "end": 649, "text": "This toponym-based name is similar to the unrelated etymology for the Tagalog people, which is derived from \"taga-\" (\"person from\") and \"ilog\" (\"river\") The Ibanag language (also Ybanag) is spoken by about 500,000 speakers in two of the northeasternmost provinces"}, "D5S1": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d04", "start": 0, "end": 601, "text": "move to sidebar hide Actions Read Edit View history General What links here Related changes Upload file Permanent link Page information Cite this page Get shortened URL Download QR code Print/export Create a book Download as PDF Printable version In other projects Appearance move to sidebar hide From Wiktionary , the free dictionary See also : ibanag English [ edit ] English Wikipedia has an article on : Ibanag language Wikipedia English Wikipedia has an article on : Ibanag people Wikipedia Noun [ edit ] Ibanag pl ( plural only ) An ethnolinguistic minority inhabiting the provinces of Cagayan ,"}}, "candidates": [{"candidate_id": "C0", "answer": "Ibanag language (also \"Ybanag\" or \"Ibanak\") spoken by the Ibanag people", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d00:c0"]}, {"candidate_id": "C1", "answer": "a type of traditional dance performed during cultural festivals and celebrations", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d02:c0"]}, {"candidate_id": "C2", "answer": "an ethnolinguistic minority numbering a little more than half a million people, who inhabit the provinces of Cagayan, Isabela and Nueva Vizcaya", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d03:c0"]}, {"candidate_id": "C3", "answer": "An ethnolinguistic minority inhabiting the provinces of Cagayan", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0264-02dc3d4e1276:d04:c0"]}], "original_cards": [{"doc_id":